course: dictionary
lesson: 11458
title: Formal words in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up because they belong to law, rules and exams. «Admissible» is one of them. Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «The judge said the photo was admissible evidence.» Evidence is proof, and a judge decides what proof is allowed, so admissible must mean allowed.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence to check it. «Only tickets bought before nine o'clock are admissible.» If admissible means allowed in, the sentence makes sense: only those tickets are accepted at the gate.
explain: Everyday people often use simpler words for the same idea. A teacher might say «allowed» or «acceptable» instead of admissible. Knowing the simple words helps you understand the formal ones.
fact: Admissible means allowed or acceptable.
fact: In law, admissible means allowed to be used as evidence in a court.
fact: Admissible can also mean allowed to enter or be accepted.
fact: «The judge said the note was admissible evidence.» This means the judge let the note be used in court.
fact: Formal words like admissible are often used in law, rules and exams. Everyday words like allowed or acceptable mean nearly the same thing.
exercise: «The judge said the note was admissible evidence.» What does admissible mean here? | Hidden under the bed | Lost forever | Too old to read | *Allowed to be used in court
exercise: «Only tickets bought before nine o'clock are admissible.» What does admissible mean? | Sold out | *Allowed in | Very expensive | Torn or damaged
exercise: A clue that breaks the rules may not be ___ in court. Which word fits the gap best? | Glum | *Admissible | Luminous | Ancient
test: The referee said the goal was not admissible, so it did not count. What does not admissible mean? | Made of wood | Very fast | Scored twice | *Not allowed
test: Mum says the ticket is admissible at the gate. What does that tell you? | It is torn up | It is too small | It is lost | *It is accepted, so you can go in
exam: «Only evidence that follows the rules is admissible in court.» What does admissible mean? | *Allowed or acceptable | Hidden from everyone | Heavy and large | Sad and tired
